title: Interactive command arguments
tags: Commands
kind: reference
aliases: arguments args prompt argspec
summary: A command run without all its arguments asks for the missing ones in the command input, one at a time, each with its own completions.

A command that takes arguments ''declares'' them — the shell's builtins as much as
the panels' commands. When an invocation supplies fewer than declared, whether it
comes from a key, the command input or a script, the command input asks for the
missing ones in order. `panel:open` asks which setting, then which panel type;
`panel:open here` asks only the type; `panel:open here file` asks nothing. Asking is
what happens to an incomplete invocation, not a separate mode.

A wrong ''value'' is another matter: it reaches the command, which says what is wrong
with it.

!! Answering

The prompt shows the question, the line holds a starting value when there is one
(the current name for `workspace:rename`), and the list shows the candidates,
narrowed as you type like the command list.

|!Key |!Does |
|`Enter` |answers with the ''highlighted candidate'', and moves on to the next argument |
|`ctrl+Enter` |answers with the text as typed, even when a candidate is highlighted — for a new value that happens to match an existing one |
|`Up` past the first row |removes the highlight; `Enter` then answers with the text as typed |
|`Tab` |writes the highlighted candidate into the line |
|<<key "completion:cycle forward" "ctrl+,">> / <<key "completion:cycle back" "ctrl+.">> |show the candidates named another way, when there are several ([[Completion views]]) |
|`Escape` |abandons the whole invocation; nothing runs |

With no candidate offered, or none highlighted, `Enter` answers with the text as
typed. The hint at the right of the line recalls the two forms: `⏎ pick · ⌃⏎ new`.

At most 200 candidates are shown; typing narrows them.

One question at a time holds the line: while a script's question is pending, a
command that needs to ask refuses with ''the command input is busy with another
prompt'', and the reverse.

Nothing answered here goes into the [[input history|Input history]].

!! What a command declares

For whoever writes a panel or a user command — for each argument, in order:

|!Field |!Meaning |
|`name` |the argument's name |
|`prompt(prior)` |the question shown |
|`initial(prior)` |a starting value, editable |
|`complete(partial, prior)` |the candidates: one function, or an array of views ([[Completion views]]) |
|`views(prior)` |the views, when which ones exist depends on the earlier answers; wins over `complete` unless it returns none |
|`when(prior)` |whether the argument applies at all |
|`optional` |the argument may be left out, and is then never asked for |

Every function is called when the argument is reached, never at registration, and
receives the arguments collected so far — so `initial` reads the live state, and the
candidates of a value depend on the field named before it. The question opens at
once: its candidates may arrive after, so a slow list never delays the prompt.

`when` lets one command cover a family of operations. The command declares every
argument any of its operations takes, and those an operation has no use for are
dropped: `metarecord:bulk selection delete` names no field, `… unset` no value. A
dropped argument takes no word of the invocation either.

`optional` is for a trailing modifier whose absence is itself the meaning:
`workspace:next` moves both panels, `workspace:next slot` the focused one only.
Only the last arguments can be optional, since they are filled by position.

The last declared argument takes all the remaining words of the invocation.

!! Whose arguments

A panel type is built once per workspace, and every one of them registers the same
command names. The declared arguments belong to the panel ''instance'': the questions
come from the instance of the focused workspace — the one the command will run on —
so the candidates are never another workspace's. A workspace that never showed the
panel has it built before the first question.
